      "id": "INT-04",
      "pillar": "integration",
      "difficulty": "Intermediate",
      "concept": "Anti-Corruption Layer (ACL)",
      "scenario": "Your modern E-commerce Checkout domain integrates with a 15-year-old legacy ERP over SOAP XML where statuses are cryptic codes (`STAT_04_HLD`), prices are strings in local quirks, and customer IDs change format per country. Three new teams need ERP data this quarter.",
      "question": "Where should the translation logic live to protect your core domains from legacy schema churn and cryptic semantics?",
      "options": [
        "Expose the ERP SOAP models directly in your internal GraphQL schema so every frontend and microservice can map the fields it needs.",
        "Build a dedicated Anti-Corruption Layer (ACL) boundary adapter that translates ERP XML/quirks into clean, canonical domain types and events before any core service consumes them.",
        "Ask the ERP vendor to rewrite their SOAP API into clean JSON REST endpoints before starting your feature work.",
        "Replicate the ERP's internal database tables directly into every microservice's database using nightly CSV dumps."
      ],
      "correctIndex": 1,
      "explanation": "An Anti-Corruption Layer isolates legacy messy semantics, protocols, and brittle models behind a translation boundary. Core services speak clean ubiquitous domain concepts (`OrderStatus.ON_HOLD`, `Money`), while all SOAP parsing, code mapping, and ERP bug workarounds live in one replaceable adapter.",
      "distractorAnalysis": {
        "0": {
          "trap": "Spreading legacy corruption everywhere",
          "whyWrong": "Exposing raw ERP quirks to every caller duplicates fragile `if (STAT_04_HLD)` logic across multiple teams and locks the entire company to the legacy model."
        },
        "1": {
          "keyInsight": "Quarantining external volatility inside an ACL keeps core business logic clean and makes eventual ERP replacement trivial."
        },
        "2": {
          "trap": "Blocking delivery on an external dependency you don't control",
          "whyWrong": "Legacy vendor rewrites take quarters or years and carry high risk; you need architectural isolation that works against the ERP as it exists today."
        },
        "3": {
          "trap": "Coupling directly to internal physical schemas",
          "whyWrong": "CSV table dumps bypass business invariants, create stale batch windows, and couple all microservices to internal ERP table layouts."
        }
      },
      "wisdomTakeaway": "Never let an external vendor's data model become your internal domain language—translate at the border with an Anti-Corruption Layer.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Anti-corruption Layer pattern",
          "author": "Microsoft Azure Architecture Center",
          "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/anti-corruption-layer"
        },
        {
          "title": "Bounded Context",
          "author": "Martin Fowler",
          "url": "https://martinfowler.com/bliki/BoundedContext.html"
        }
      ]
