      "id": "INT-07",
      "pillar": "integration",
      "difficulty": "Intermediate",
      "concept": "Bulkhead Isolation",
      "scenario": "Your API server exposes `/search`, `/checkout`, and `/export-pdf`. The `/export-pdf` endpoint calls a slow third-party rendering service. Whenever the PDF vendor slows down to 20s responses, `/checkout` and `/search` also start timing out with 503s even though their own databases are healthy.",
      "question": "Which pattern prevents the slow PDF dependency from starving unrelated healthy endpoints?",
      "options": [
        "Bulkhead Pattern: isolate thread pools, connection pools, or semaphores per downstream dependency/workload so PDF rendering can exhaust only its own capped quota.",
        "Increase the global web server worker thread pool from 200 to 2,000.",
        "Add a global LRU response cache in front of `/export-pdf`.",
        "Move `/export-pdf` from HTTP/1.1 to HTTP/2 multiplexing on the same shared worker pool."
      ],
      "correctIndex": 0,
      "explanation": "Like watertight compartments on a ship, Bulkheads partition concurrency resources (threads, HTTP client pools, semaphore slots) per dependency or route. If the PDF vendor hangs and consumes all 15 PDF slots, new PDF requests fail fast while the 185 slots reserved for `/checkout` and `/search` remain completely unaffected.",
      "distractorAnalysis": {
        "0": {
          "keyInsight": "Resource partitioning caps the blast radius of a single slow dependency so healthy paths keep their capacity."
        },
        "1": {
          "trap": "Throwing unpartitioned capacity at a slow leak",
          "whyWrong": "Without bulkheads, 2,000 threads will simply also get consumed by slow PDF calls during sustained degradation—plus 2,000 threads increase memory footprint and CPU context switching."
        },
        "2": {
          "trap": "Assuming every slow endpoint is cacheable",
          "whyWrong": "Custom user-specific PDF exports rarely have high cache hit rates, and cache misses still saturate the shared worker pool."
        },
        "3": {
          "trap": "Confusing transport multiplexing with worker pool isolation",
          "whyWrong": "HTTP/2 multiplexes TCP frames over one socket, which doesn't stop slow PDF work from holding every application worker thread on the shared pool."
        }
      },
      "wisdomTakeaway": "Shared resource pools turn one slow external vendor into a total platform outage; isolate slow or untrusted dependencies behind dedicated bulkheads.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Bulkhead pattern",
          "author": "Microsoft Azure Architecture Center",
          "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/bulkhead"
        },
        {
          "title": "Timeouts, retries, and backoff with jitter",
          "author": "Marc Brooker (AWS Builders' Library)",
          "url": "https://builder.aws.com/content/3EumjoZascWd1oZiEgL8ORlv3qE/timeouts-retries-and-backoff-with-jitter"
        }
      ]
